### What is the Longest Common Substring problem?

The Longest Common Substring problem asks for the longest contiguous block of characters that appears in both strings. Unlike the Longest Common Subsequence, which allows gaps, a substring must be a continuous stretch. For example, for `"TRANSFER"` and `"TRANSFORM"`, the longest common substring is `"TRANS"` with length 5.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// String A: "TRANSFER"
// String B: "TRANSFORM"
// Common substrings: "T", "TR", "TRA", "TRAN", "TRANS", "R", "AN", ...
// Longest: "TRANS" with length 5
```

### How does the DP table differ from LCS?

The crucial difference is what `dp[i][j]` represents. In LCS, `dp[i][j]` is the best LCS length up to prefixes of length i and j. In Longest Common Substring, `dp[i][j]` is the length of the common run ending exactly at those two positions. This means the cell only has a nonzero value if the characters at those positions match and extend a run from the diagonal.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// LCS dp[i][j]:     best subsequence length for prefixes i and j
// Substring dp[i][j]: length of common run ENDING at A[i-1] and B[j-1]

// LCS mismatch:     dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])
// Substring mismatch: dp[i][j] = 0  (run is broken)

// LCS answer:       dp[m][n] (bottom-right corner)
// Substring answer: max over ALL cells in the table
```

### How do you implement it?

You build a table of size `(m+1) x (n+1)` with a zero border. You fill it row by row, and on each match you extend the diagonal by one. On each mismatch you set the cell to zero. You track a running maximum over all cells as you fill them.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LongestCommonSubstring {

    public int findLength(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, dp[i][j]);
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = 0;
                }
            }
        }
        return maxLen;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LongestCommonSubstring solver = new LongestCommonSubstring();
        System.out.println(solver.findLength("TRANSFER", "TRANSFORM"));
        System.out.println(solver.findLength("ABCDE", "ABFCE"));
    }
}
```

```text
5
2
```

### How do you reconstruct the actual substring?

To extract the actual substring, track the ending index alongside the maximum length. When you find a new maximum, record the current position. The substring is the last `maxLen` characters ending at that position.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LongestCommonSubstringWithReconstruction {

    public String findSubstring(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];
        int maxLen = 0;
        int endIndexA = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                    if (dp[i][j] > maxLen) {
                        maxLen = dp[i][j];
                        endIndexA = i;
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        return a.substring(endIndexA - maxLen, endIndexA);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LongestCommonSubstringWithReconstruction solver =
            new LongestCommonSubstringWithReconstruction();
        System.out.println(solver.findSubstring("TRANSFER", "TRANSFORM"));
    }
}
```

```text
TRANS
```

### How do you optimize space with a rolling row?

Since each cell only depends on the diagonal cell from the previous row, you can use a single 1D array and fill it from right to left to avoid overwriting the diagonal value before it is read. This reduces space from O(m * n) to O(n).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LongestCommonSubstringOptimized {

    public int findLength(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[] prev = new int[n + 1];
        int maxLen = 0;

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            int[] curr = new int[n + 1];
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    curr[j] = prev[j - 1] + 1;
                    maxLen = Math.max(maxLen, curr[j]);
                }
                // else curr[j] stays 0
            }
            prev = curr;
        }
        return maxLen;
    }
}
```

### How is this different from Longest Common Subsequence?

This is the most common interview trap. Both use a 2D table with the same match rule (diagonal + 1), but they differ in the mismatch rule and where the answer is found.

| Aspect | Longest Common Substring | Longest Common Subsequence |
|---|---|---|
| Contiguity | Must be contiguous | Gaps allowed |
| Mismatch rule | dp[i][j] = 0 (reset) | dp[i][j] = max(top, left) |
| Answer location | Global max over all cells | Bottom-right corner dp[m][n] |
| Cell meaning | Run length ending here | Best length up to here |

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Example: A = "ABCDE", B = "ABFCE"
// Longest Common Substring: "AB" (length 2) - must be contiguous
// LCS: "ABCE" (length 4) - gaps allowed between A,B,C,E
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, a forensics tool compares two server request logs to find the longest identical contiguous block that might indicate a replayed request batch. Because a replay must be a contiguous identical run, the substring DP is the right choice.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ReplayDetector {

    private final LongestCommonSubstringWithReconstruction solver =
        new LongestCommonSubstringWithReconstruction();

    public String findReplayedBlock(String log1, String log2) {
        String block = solver.findSubstring(log1, log2);
        if (block.length() >= 5) {
            System.out.println("ALERT: Possible replay detected: " + block);
        }
        return block;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ReplayDetector detector = new ReplayDetector();
        String log1 = "ABCWDTRANSFERWDX";
        String log2 = "XYTRANSFERWDABC";
        detector.findReplayedBlock(log1, log2);
    }
}
```

```text
ALERT: Possible replay detected: TRANSFERWD
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is confusing this with LCS and using `max(top, left)` on mismatch instead of resetting to zero. Another mistake is looking for the answer at `dp[m][n]` instead of tracking the global maximum. A third mistake is forgetting to explicitly set cells to zero on mismatch when the array is not pre-initialized.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: using LCS mismatch rule
if (a.charAt(i-1) == b.charAt(j-1)) {
    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;
} else {
    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]); // WRONG for substring!
}

// CORRECT: reset to zero on mismatch
if (a.charAt(i-1) == b.charAt(j-1)) {
    dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1;
} else {
    dp[i][j] = 0; // contiguous run is broken
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that for very long strings, suffix arrays or suffix trees can solve this in O(m + n) time, though they are more complex to implement. The rolling-row DP is the practical middle ground for interviews. The substring problem also has applications in plagiarism detection, DNA sequence analysis, and log forensics.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Senior notes:
// 1. Suffix array/tree: O(m+n) for longest common substring
// 2. Rolling row: O(n) space, sufficient for interviews
// 3. Key trap: substring vs subsequence mismatch rule
// 4. Applications: plagiarism detection, DNA analysis, log replay detection
// 5. Track ending index alongside max length for reconstruction
```
